rcq("R02-D-08-2", "Author's tone / attitude", "Medium",
    "The author's attitude toward the proposal that part of the slowing reflects a larger store of knowledge is best described as",
    "qualified interest: doubtful that it accounts for every loss, but valuing what it shows about interpretation.",
    [("firm endorsement, on the ground that it shows apparent decline to be simply the cost of accumulated knowledge.",
      "Paragraph 3 says the proposal 'is contested, and it cannot explain away every loss', which rules out firm endorsement."),
     ("dismissive scepticism, on the ground that the proposal is contested and cannot account for every loss.",
      "The author goes on: 'It does, however, show' that a slower response can be read two ways, so the proposal is valued, not dismissed."),
     ("strict neutrality, reporting the proposal without offering any judgement about its worth or its limits.",
      "The author judges both its limits ('cannot explain away every loss') and its value ('It does, however, show'), so the stance is not neutral.")],
    "Paragraph 3 reports the proposal, notes 'The proposal is contested, and it cannot explain away every loss', and then says 'It does, however, show' that the same observation can be read as deterioration or as a cost of accumulation.",
    "Seizing on the reservation and missing the 'however' that follows it.",
    "Two wrong options each take one half of a two-sided judgement.",
    cognitive="Understanding")

rcq("R02-D-08-3", "Inference", "Difficult",
    "It can be inferred from paragraph 2 that studies following the same people over decades",
    "may understate the decline that occurs in the population at large, because those who remain in them tend to be healthier and more able.",
    [("give the more accurate estimate of when decline begins, because they remove the effect of differences in schooling between generations.",
      "They do remove that difference, but paragraph 2 adds that they 'carry biases of their own' and that 'Neither method gives a clean view', so the author withholds this ranking."),
     ("confirm that the decline in rapid processing begins in early adulthood and continues steadily, just as the familiar picture holds.",
      "Paragraph 2 says such studies 'tend to show later and gentler declines than such snapshots suggest', which is not a confirmation of the familiar picture."),
     ("overstate decline, because the people who take part become less interested in performing well as the years go by.",
      "Waning interest is never mentioned, and the bias the passage does name, healthier and abler people staying in, would push the other way.")],
    "Paragraph 2 says that 'those who stay in a long study are often healthier and more able than those who drop out'. If the remaining sample is healthier and abler, the decline it shows is likely to be smaller than the decline in everyone, so such studies may understate it.",
    "Choosing the 'more accurate' option because it is half right about schooling, while missing that the author refuses to rank the methods.",
    "Requires working out the direction of a bias the passage names but does not spell out, against a half-true wrong option.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=CE)

rcq("R02-D-08-4", "Strengthen / weaken / assumption", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following findings, if true, would most strengthen the proposal described in paragraph 3?",
    "A computer model of word retrieval, given ever larger vocabularies with its processing unchanged, slowed down in the way that older adults do.",
    [("Older adults score higher than younger adults on tests of vocabulary and general knowledge drawn from everyday life.",
      "Paragraph 3 already reports this; it shows that the store is larger but not that a larger store causes slower responses, which is what the proposal claims."),
     ("Slowing in older adults is just as marked on tasks that call on no stored knowledge as on tasks requiring the retrieval of words.",
      "If slowing is equal where no store is searched, the store cannot be its cause; this weakens the proposal rather than strengthening it."),
     ("Younger adults who know two languages well name pictured objects as quickly as younger adults who know only one language.",
      "A larger vocabulary here brings no slowing, which counts against the proposal; it is relevant, but in the wrong direction.")],
    "The proposal is that slowing partly reflects 'a larger store': a mind that knows more words 'takes longer to find any one of them'. A model in which only vocabulary size changes, and retrieval slows as in ageing, isolates store size as a sufficient cause, which supports the proposal.",
    "Choosing the vocabulary finding because it fits the proposal's premise, without seeing that it does not connect store size to slowing.",
    "Two wrong options bear on the proposal but weaken it; the third supports a premise but not the causal link, so the solver must test each against the precise claim.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=CE)

rcq("R02-D-08-5", "Except / not supported", "Difficult",
    "The passage presents each of the following as a shortcoming of the familiar picture of the ageing mind EXCEPT that it",
    "fails to recognise the serenity and sound judgement that age reliably brings to those who reach it.",
    [("rests heavily on comparisons that mix the effects of age with differences between generations.",
      "Paragraph 2 says one-time comparisons confound age with 'less schooling, different work and less familiarity with the kind of test'."),
     ("overlooks the knowledge that goes on accumulating well into later life and on which older adults often excel.",
      "Paragraph 3 says the familiar picture 'attends to only half of what changes', omitting knowledge that 'goes on accumulating'."),
     ("treats a slower response as necessarily a sign of deterioration, when it might be a cost of accumulation.",
      "Paragraph 3 says a slower response 'can be read as a symptom of deterioration or as a cost of accumulation', which the familiar picture ignores.")],
    "Paragraph 4 rejects the 'narrative of wisdom' and says 'Older people are not uniformly wise', so the author does not fault the decline picture for missing a serenity that age reliably brings. The other three shortcomings are stated in paragraphs 2 and 3.",
    "Assuming that because the author criticises the decline picture, the author must endorse its opposite.",
    "The correct answer is an attractive opposite of the criticised view; spotting it needs paragraph 4's rejection of the wisdom narrative.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=CE)
